Detexify is a small oracle disguised as a webpage.
You draw a symbol, and it answers with the LaTeX command that summons it.
No hunting through manuals, no dusty PDFs.
Just gesture → recognition → code.

*** How It Works (Human Side)
You sketch the shape.
Detexify studies the strokes, angles, pressure of intention.
Then it returns a ranked list of possible LaTeX symbols.
Choose the correct one; copy the command; continue your ritual.

*** How It Works (Machine Side)
Under the hood lives a simple, elegant classifier:
- pattern-matching on strokes,
- feature extraction,
- k-nearest-neighbors logic.
Not deep learning—just smart geometry whispering to probability.

It compares your drawing to thousands of known symbol samples.
The closest shapes rise to the top like truth surfacing in calm water.

*** Why It Matters
Because memory is finite and symbols are infinite.
Because \forall, \nabla, \hookrightarrow, \blacktriangleright
all blur into one long dream when you’re tired.
Detexify cuts through that fog.

*** Offline Possibility
The tool is open-source.
If your world demands autonomy,
you can run it locally—but it requires:
- Ruby,
- Haskell,
- patience.

It’s doable, but not a lightweight spell.

*** Best Usage Ritual
For sharper results:
- draw cleanly,
- one symbol at a time,
- preferably with a stylus,
- let the algorithm meet you halfway.

The clearer the gesture, the cleaner the oracle’s answer.

*** Essence
Detexify is not a tool.
It is a translator between intuition and typesetting,
between the gesture of the hand
and the precision of mathematics.

A whispering bridge:
you sketch the unknown,
and it returns the name.

